   209	### New canon minted (author's list; all ruled KEEP)
   210	- **Lowmarch's ground (flag):** the town on the river's east bank; a cable ferry, a square scow wound across by a drum in a round thatched capstan-shed, with a beast walking the circle; a copper a wheel and a copper a beast; the lower inn with a deep eave, above the landing; the upper inn on the rise, with two chimneys; the smith between; the meadow below, half a mile, with one great ash; standing-money a copper a night, hay by the net; the grass behind the landing. Roads out: the west road, the north water road, the east road over a saddle (a cart's width to the saddle, a mule's gauge beyond), the south road (it forks), and the cold road, which leaves the north road at a dead tree.
   211	- **The plank (flag):** twelve feet of tarred ship's oak on two posts under the lower inn's eave; slips on bark, hide, a shingle or paper, each on one tack, with a small picture beside the words for people without letters; Pike's horn ink-pot set in a bored hole; his flat river stone for the page; an iron box bolted under the plank for the hirers' coin.
   212	- **How a line is made (flag):** the price is left with Pike at posting; a crew takes a slip at the plank and Pike marks it; the job's witness marks the slip; Pike reads the line aloud before he writes it and again after, then pays. Pike's sign for the five: five short strokes. Marks seen: the ferryman's (a ring with a dot), the north-water steading's (three uprights and a bar), the hide-woman's (a square with four pegs), Thornwater's (a thorn twig over a wavy line), the landing broker's (a hook and a bar, in wax and burned on his goods).
   213	- **The wall (flag):** one sheet of sized sailcloth pinned by the lower inn's hearth, in Pike's hand, a line to a crew. Thirteen lines on day 49. One crew is entered under its mule's name (not given).
   214	- **HOLD (flag):** a captain asks Pike to keep a slip by until he is back; "Anybody can ask. I've said yes to one."
   215	- **Pike:** the left arm gone at the elbow, sleeve pinned with a brass pin; forty headings in the book; "thirty years" of the same question. His guess that the top crew's captain is Bronze, unwritten; the nearest station "nine days west".
   216	- **The sale (#23; flag):** the gelding to the ferryman, for his capstan, with dry room for the three boxes in the loft over the drum as part of the price; the old capstan mule put out to grass. The cart to the smith, who means to cut its axle to mule gauge and hire it out. Brom's bill-hook came under the cart's seat board. Lira's long knife is the last thing the Book 6 purse buys. No figure is given for the sale, the purse or the reserve.
   217	- **The reserve:** sewn into canvas by Karis, waxed by Brom, five thumbs in the wax, at the bottom of the third box under the masonry chips; opened by all five or not at all.
   218	- **The hold, as taught (flag):** the gravel bar drills; "you refuse at the first stride and at every stride after"; the word **rent** for what a fragment costs to carry idle. Karis habitually leaves set points behind her and held one unlit to ninety, later three hundred. Brom's Iron Skin comes "all or none".
   219	- **Shadow's source habit (flag):** Seln learned it on a stair as a boy; "a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident"; it came at dusk. Two drifts in Cael (day 5, four strides; day 24, one stride), logged as rent.
   220	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the courier (about fifty, a split lip long healed, a hat cut away on the right, a hatchet in a saddle loop); the three carts' people, who are one group throughout: the old carter with two mules, who takes the shade of the ash; the boy of about fifteen; the hide-woman, who is also the wash's hirer; the ferryman and his boy; the standing-money boy; the smith; the landing broker and his clerk; the old man of the north-water steading, his white dog, his goat; the Thornwater carter (short, thick, twenty-two years on his road); the steading's woman (broad, a grey braid); the bowman; the north-road woman captain (a red cloth round her hair; two men and a girl); the eleven-bale captain (a new coat).
   221	- **The road's craft:** two cairns set to mark a slanting ford; stock watered below the crossing; the stone rings on halting-grounds are for chocks, not fires; a carter's axles kept in mutton fat, trace chains bound in sacking, heel-plates cut off.
   222	- **Fauna:** red-barked conifers with flat paired needles (Karis will not name them; "red not-pine" is entered as Lira's); a broad pale tree with silver-backed leaves; grey long-legged deer with white rumps that are not afraid of people.
   223	- **The wash (flag):** three hours out on the east road; twelve feet deep and thirty across; a ramp on each side; a thorn stump on the eastern lip.
   224	- **The stretch (flag):** a mile of chest-high pale grass between two shoulders of hill; frogs in a wet bottom; one split cart-shaft standing in the grass; a stony swell on the right at the half mile; a gravel fan a hundred feet wide from a side gully; a white stone a hundred and ten of Cael's strides from the gate.
   225	- **The stillhound, as seen:** grey, low, long in the back, calf-high at the shoulder, head carried level; silent in grass; the frogs stop ahead of it and start behind; prints as broad as a palm.
   226	- **Thornwater (flag):** a grain hold: river-country grain stored and fetched by carts from holds farther east. A fence of split posts as high as a man's head, a quarter of a mile about; a west gate with a bar and a platform beside it; a wicket north to grazing and a stone hut; an east gate and a road on up the valley (walked by Seln for two hours, at noon; nothing said of it); a long house, a barn with the loft over it, a cart-yard, a well with a sweep, geese.
   227	- **The carter's map (flag):** a scraped calfskin in oak-gall ink and burnt stick: roads as double lines, fords as three wavy strokes, holds as a square with a dot, Lowmarch as a ring with a bar, days as ticks, and bare hide wherever a wheel has no business. Given to Karis for the wheel Brom rebuilt. Five roads run off its edges.
   228	- **Teague, as seen (flag):** forty or so, middle height, heavy in the forearm, cropped hair grey over the ears; a black leather band laced on the right wrist; a hand-axe in his belt; a coat mended by somebody else. His week on the cold road is salt on mules to the top holds before frost shuts the pass, along two miles of ledge; he has done it nine times. He thinks only "the spear", "the bow" (a bad leg), "the boy". One other crew on the sheet could clear the ford and is up the north road.
   229	
   230	### State at movement end (author; updated after repair r1)
   231	
   232	*Updated after repair r1 (see `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md`). Facts changed: the gate-to-stone distance is a hundred and ten YARDS (Cael paced it at a yard to the stride; Seln's hundred and four longer paces agree with it); the animal "tracked the buckle" and no sense is named anywhere; Pike's Thornwater line is printed once, in chapter 6, and its saying in chapter 7 is narration; the twelve-second doorway hold is recalled at Thornwater's ladder, not in the day-18 inventory. Word counts, metrics and line numbers elsewhere in this report are the pre-repair figures.*
   233	
   234	**Calendar.** The evening of the **forty-ninth day past the Line**, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page; "Fifth-day" appears twice (ch4, ch5) as the academy's own word. Days on the page: 2 (night), 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10–12, 14, 15–21 (drills; the catalogue on the 18th), 22–24, 30 (Seln, the cold road), 33, 41, 42, 43, 44–46, 48, 49. Weather: warm, dusty, long light; the roads "dry right through" from about day 46; one storm over the eastern ridge on day 33. The next movement opens on day 51.
   235	
   236	**Where everyone is.** All five at one table in the lower inn, under the wall, with one jug. They sleep in the meadow. Teague and his three are on the sill of the same room and go out on the cold road at first light on day 50 for a week.
   237	
   238	**Cael.** Eighteen, said to Pike on day 9. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly** (*Still open. Still real. Patience.*, written on day 18). **Deployment since the Line: none.** No injury; the forearm is "sound again" by day 5 and is not mentioned afterward. Idle states: Shadow sealed, costing from waking, drifting at dusk (two drifts, logged as rent); Anchor asking at every sill, gate, rim and rail; Blade asking *Edge?* and taking names; Storm heavy; Wind leaning his feet. He has not read the Iron read once. The log has an entry for most days, each ending on the deployment line.
   239	
   240	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One, the certificate in her coat. Uninjured (palms skinned at the wash on day 33, healed by day 41). One burst went off by itself on the gravel bar in the first week of drills; none since. She can stop dead from a full run. She wears a long knife bought at Lowmarch's smith. She has been noticed, once, by Thornwater's bowman.
   241	
   242	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper "for good", said to Pike. Uninjured. Iron Skin came on the bar in drills and started and was put down at the ferry, the wash and the stretch; it did not come at all on the ladder. He carries the hedging bill. He holds the board purse's coin only when it is paid into his hand; Karis keeps it.
   243	
   244	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Uninjured. Sets no points on a road. Holds: the field archive; a leaf headed *stillhound*, underlined once, with five counts and *110 yards* (*gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped*); the entry of her own not-asking (day 22); the five column heads with *Outcome* underlined twice; Pike's copied line; **the carter's map**. She has asked nobody what the animal is waiting for.
   245	
   246	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze, said once at a fire. Uninjured. Has not lifted his Path since the crossroads fire. Has walked the first hours of eight roads, five out of Lowmarch and three out of Thornwater; on day 49 he says, in one sentence, that he has walked the first part of all five out of Lowmarch. Has the prints and the forty-five-yard line by the white stone in his head, a hundred and ten yards from the gate (a hundred and four of his own paces), and has told nobody. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.**
   247	
   248	**Objects and money.** Three numbered boxes, in the meadow with them on day 49 (they live in the loft over the ferryman's drum when the crew is near; they rode the Thornwater carter's cart both ways). **The cart and the gelding are sold** (day 12). **The reserve** is sealed at the bottom of the third box and has not been opened. Board purse: **eighteen marks earned** (ferry 2, fence 4, wash 3, Thornwater road 9); **about ten in hand** after one jug. Twenty pounds of flour from the steading, mostly eaten. The two letters to Hesk and Vell went west on day 4, a copper each to the turf fire. Hesk's satchel holds the log, the old volume, the observation notebook, and Pike's line on a scrap of bark. The ash wedge is still with them. The registry's printed sheet is in the middle box and was not unrolled.
   249	
   250	**Pike's book and the wall.** A heading of five names; **four lines**: *Ferry, the landing's goods. Six crossings stood. Nothing lost. Two marks.* (day 14); the fence (forty posts cut and set; four marks and flour; the saying is not on the page); the wash (three marks; not on the page); and the protected Thornwater line (day 49). **Thirteenth of thirteen; eleven lines between theirs and the top.** The top line is *Teague.* On the plank: *Thornwater ford. Clearance. Forty marks.* with *HOLD. Teague.* in Pike's capitals.
   251	
   252	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five, and nobody else. Pike has not asked what Cael is, and has said that he never asked. Three people have called him paper and been told yes. The party knows the word *stillhound* and nothing of what it hunts by, or by what sense it followed a buckle; it has Cael's five counts, Seln's unshared reading of the ground, and the country's rule without its reason. **Nobody has heard the words "quiet ground".** Teague has read the five and found Seln on his second pass; he cannot tell what Cael is. Cael has read Teague. They have not spoken.
   253	
   254	**Open threads.** What a road hears. What the grey thing waits for. Why a hundred and ten. Why it will not cross wet gravel. The forty-mark slip, held a week for a crew that leaves at dawn, on a stretch that took two carts last month. Lira with no ladder. Brom's first earned coin. Karis's price for not asking. Seln's eight roads, waiting to bear on a day. The second question nobody put to the steading's woman. Vell, of whom Pike has never heard. The hook and the bar.
   255	
   256	### Movement 2 inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into MOVEMENT-002's packet)
   257	Movement 2 must inherit these surviving-prose facts in addition to its own explicit entry paragraph:
   258	
   259	- **Road culture is contextual:** ordinary meadow/yard use is open, while travel roads and exposed fence-lines trigger the hold. Do not retroactively make Lowmarch's meadow silent or Pathless (`manuscript/chapter-02.md:55-63`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:165-183`).
   260	- **The companions have failed before succeeding at the hold:** Lira has one involuntary practice burst, Brom has let Iron Skin rise and put it down, and Karis has lit at ninety and later held unlit to three hundred. These are native-Path events, not additions to Cael's deployment count (`manuscript/chapter-04.md:21-65`; `manuscript/chapter-05.md:7`).
   261	- **The stillhound file is divided:** Karis has the name, five observations and 110; Seln privately has palm-wide prints, the forty-five-yard follower spacing and an explicitly unknown signal; neither has the discharge mechanism (`manuscript/chapter-05.md:221-257`; `manuscript/chapter-06.md:107-139,217-223`).
   262	- **Lowmarch's working geography and institutions persist:** cable ferry/capstan, lower inn/eave/plank, upper inn, smith, meadow/ash, road fan, public read-before/read-after procedure, iron payment box, sailcloth wall and Pike's double look (`manuscript/chapter-02.md:13-87,145-163`; `manuscript/chapter-03.md:251-277`; `manuscript/chapter-07.md:29-63,181-219`).
   263	- **Asset state after #23:** no cart or horse; the reserve is sealed under masonry chips in the third box and opens only by all five; the boxes lodge over the ferry drum when the crew is near Lowmarch; Seln's closed case is back on its strap (`manuscript/chapter-03.md:107,123-185`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:277`; `manuscript/chapter-05.md:55-65`).
   264	- **Carried tools and papers:** Brom has the hedging bill and ash wedge; Lira has the long knife; Karis has the dry carter's map and the still-rolled registry sheet; Hesk's satchel has the logs, notebook and Pike's bark line, while the Hesk/Vell letters have gone west (`manuscript/chapter-03.md:123-129`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:311-331`; `manuscript/chapter-06.md:165-169,195-223`; `manuscript/chapter-01.md:159-197`).
   265	- **Local relationship continuity:** the hide-woman is the same person who gave the cake and hired the wash recovery; Thornwater's woman, bowman and carter have already seen the crew hold; the carter gave Karis his twenty-two-year map in exchange for Brom's wheel repair (`manuscript/chapter-02.md:3-5`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:285-353`; `manuscript/chapter-06.md:13-55,187-211`).
   266	- **Board texture at handoff:** four earned lines, thirteenth of thirteen with eleven between them and Teague, Thornwater's ford held under Teague's name, and Pike's plank physically crowded but not count-fixed (`manuscript/chapter-07.md:103-123,181-227,271-277`).
   267	- **Teague's day-49 read:** he counts only spear, bow and boy; the bow has a bad leg; he knows the Thornwater road and considers its ford worse than the stretch; he finds Seln on the second pass and cannot tell what Cael is. He and Cael have not spoken (`manuscript/chapter-07.md:299-353,359-379`).
   268	- **Bodies at handoff:** all five are uninjured; Lira's wash palms have skinned over, Cael's forearm is sound, and no healer has intervened (`manuscript/chapter-05.md:3-7`; `manuscript/chapter-01.md:235`; `manuscript/chapter-07.md:271-277`).
   269	
   270	**Movement 1 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Sol recheck r1: CLOSE, no line fixes. Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected / 17 protected. 37200 words.

