     1	# Chapter 8 — A Third
     2	
     3	A mule came over the saddle at a trot, and Cael was on his feet before he knew what had woken him.
     4	
     5	It was the pace that had done it. He had lived fifty days in a country where nothing with hooves went faster than a man could walk beside it, and the sound of four small feet coming down a hill in a hurry went through the meadow's morning like a pan dropped on a hearth. The mist was still on the river. Three fires had been lit, and at each of them somebody had stopped with a stick in his hand and was looking east.
     6	
     7	"That's the hold's," said Brom.
     8	
     9	He was sitting on his blanket with one boot on, and he had needed only a glance.
    10	
    11	"You can't know a mule at half a mile."
    12	
    13	"I know that one. She leans. I had her head on my shoulder every time I stood still in that yard." He pulled on the other boot. "And there's a boy up. He's too light for her, and she's telling him so."
    14	
    15	The boy rode straight down through the tents. A person coming in from a road went round the carts, by the river side, at a walk; this one came between them, and a woman snatched a pan out of his way, and not one voice was raised at him, which Cael noticed. The mule was dark with sweat to the girth and carried her head low. He saw the rider's face as they went by the ash, and he knew it. It was a brown boy of twelve or so, who had been sent for a hammer and a billet of ash on the morning a rung broke, and had come back with both at a run.
    16	
    17	He was not running now. He got down under the long eave with his legs gone stiff, and held to the saddle until they would bear him, and then he went and stood at the plank.
    18	
    19	Nobody was at it, and the book was not out. The slips hung in their row along the tarred oak, and the boy looked down the whole length of them without reading, as someone looks who has no letters and has been told what a thing is like. He found it by the right-hand post. Cael watched him put up his hand and lay one finger on a piece of real paper, low down, on a thorn twig with a wavy line under it.
    20	
    21	"Get up," said Cael.
    22	
    23	"I'm up," said Lira, who was not; and then she saw his face, and was.
    24	
    25	Karis already had her hand on the knot over the middle box. Seln stood a little apart with the strap across his chest, and Cael could not have said when he had risen.
    26	
    27	There was one other man in the meadow who had not gone back to his fire. The Thornwater carter had loaded yesterday, forty sacks under a net, and he had been buckling a trace when the mule came by him. He stood now with the leather hanging from his fist, and he was looking at the boy's back as a man looks at a door behind which somebody is ill.
    28	
    29	The inn door opened, and Pike came out in his shirt.
    30	
    31	Cael had never seen him so. The coat with the pinned sleeve was a thing he put on to keep the book, as a smith puts on an apron, and without it the left sleeve of his shirt hung empty from the elbow and stirred a little as he walked. He had the book under the other arm. He did not hurry, and he did not call out to ask the boy's business; he came down to the plank and set the book on it, and bent his bald brown head, since the boy was small.
    32	
    33	It was too far to hear. The boy said what he had been sent to say, and it took him three breaths, and he said it to the mark on the paper. Then he said it over again at the same length. Somebody at a gate had made him learn it by heart, and had made him promise to give it twice, to a man who took everything twice.
    34	
    35	Pike straightened, and looked down the meadow.
    36	
    37	He went over it once, the tents and the carts and the three fires, where thirteen crews were getting their breakfasts or pretending to; and then he went over it a second time, more slowly. On the second pass his eyes came to five people standing by a dead fire with their blankets still on the grass. They rested there, and went on to the end of the meadow, and came back.
    38	
    39	Then he reached up, and took hold of the tack in the slip that said *HOLD*, and worked it out of the oak.
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	"Ford's had a third."
    44	
    45	He said it to the slip, which lay on the plank under his palm, and then he looked up and said it again to the five of them, since they were the only people who had come.
    46	
    47	"A third what?" said Lira.
    48	
    49	"The third she's lost on that road since it dried. Two carts last month, and the people with them. Now a man of her own." Pike touched the thorn mark. "She sends two down every year when the water drops, to set the white stones along the lip of her ford, so that a wheel can see where the rock stops. They went at noon on the forty-ninth, and they were late coming away. One of them walked in at her gate after dark."
    50	
    51	"And the other?"
    52	
    53	"He's the third."
    54	
    55	The boy had not turned round. He stood with the mule's rein in both hands, and his shoulders were up about his ears.
    56	
    57	Cael counted back, for he could not help it. On the forty-ninth, at dusk, he had been sitting in a bar of red light with a pencil, writing a sentence that he had waited forty-nine days to trust; and two days' walk to the east, at about the same hour, a man who set stones for other people's wheels had been late at a river.
    58	
    59	"He set them every year I've used that ford," said the carter. He had come up behind them with his hat in his hand, and he spoke to the plank. "Twenty-two years. I never once put a wheel off that rock."
    60	
    61	Pike nodded to him, as one tradesman nods to another. He put the flat stone on the slip's corner and dipped his pen, and held it over the paper while he did his sum aloud, so that the meadow should have it.
    62	
    63	"He went out at yesterday's first light. He's a day and a half up the cold road by now, on a ledge, and I've nobody to send after him who'd find him. That's five days more till he's down, and two from here to her fence. A week." He looked at the carter. "How long will the east carts wait on her grain?"
    64	
    65	"They come when the roads dry. They're coming now."
    66	
    67	"And they'll want that road."
    68	
    69	"There's no other."
    70	
    71	Pike wrote. He drew one line through the name, level and thin, so that the name could still be read beneath it; and he left the word in capitals standing as it was.
    72	
    73	"It's not unwritten," he said, to nobody in particular. "It's struck. Anyone who reads that can see whose it was, and who took it off him, and which morning."
    74	
    75	He turned his shoulder to the grass and raised his voice, and it went as far as the ash.
    76	
    77	"Thornwater ford. Clearance. Forty marks. The hold on it's struck, and it's me that's struck it. It's on the plank."
    78	
    79	Nobody in the meadow moved. Cael saw a big man in a new coat find something that wanted his attention in a mule's near hind foot. A woman with a red cloth round her hair would have stood up, he thought, but she had gone up the north road two days ago with her three, and there was no other line on that sheet that Pike's eyes had rested on.
    80	
    81	He set out the claim, since it steadied him. Forty marks were offered for a ford that the first name on the wall had asked to keep; and under that he had a road that he had walked four times, and five counts and a distance on a leaf in Karis's coat. And he had the hold, which was fifty days of declaring nothing on a road, and which a carter of twenty-two years had said he would as soon have as anything. It was not much to put under a claim of that size. He was still weighing it when a mild voice spoke at the far end of the plank.
    82	
    83	"I have something that bears on this," said Seln. "I should have preferred a table."
    84	
    85	They all turned. He had his hands folded in front of him, like a clerk who has been asked for a figure.
    86	
    87	"On the forty-fifth day I went out to the white stone, at noon. The ground there had kept the animal's feet. It stood beside that stone from the time the gate opened until the bar went across, and when it left, it went by the line it had come on; and at three places in the grass I paced the distance between its line and our ruts. Forty-five yards, at each." He considered the slip. "That is how I was taught to follow a person. One is given a distance, and one keeps it, and one waits upon a signal. In a mile we did not give it one. I think that the people who die at that ford have given it, and I do not know what it is."
    88	
    89	"You've had that since the forty-fifth day," said Lira.
    90	
    91	"It did not bear until this morning."
    92	
    93	Karis was looking at him as a hungry person looks at a loaf. She had spent a fortnight with one leaf, and a second witness to it had been sleeping six feet from her boxes the whole time.
    94	
    95	"Hands," said Cael.
    96	
    97	Karis's went up first, which it had never done. Brom's followed, and Seln's. Lira had hers at her shoulder already.
    98	
    99	"I'd have had it up before he'd the tack out," she said. "I thought I ought to look as if I'd considered."
   100	
   101	Cael raised his own, and then he reached across the oak and took up the slip.
   102	
   103	Pike looked at five hands, and looked again. He drew the paper back under his stone and wrote beneath the struck name: five short strokes, side by side, like a tally with its bar left off.
   104	
   105	"That's an Iron, Rank One," said Lira, "and an Iron, Rank Three, and a Copper. And two that haven't said. In case you wondered what you'd sent."
   106	
   107	"I didn't," said Pike. "I've no column for it."
   108	
   109	"Is five enough?" said Brom.
   110	
   111	"That's arithmetic I can't do yet."
   112	
   113	He blotted it. He did not seem troubled, and he did not make light of it either; he had been asked for a sum and had given the honest state of his working.
   114	
   115	Cael watched the feather go back behind his ear, and understood what he had just seen done. That book was thirty years of a single question, which was who had come back, and every crew Pike had ever sent down a road had either proved him right or was not there to prove him wrong. In all that time he had said yes to one hold. This morning he had struck it with his own pen, before two hundred people, and written beneath it the mark of the lowest line on his wall; and if five strokes did not come up from that river, it would not be Teague's name that the meadow remembered beside the forty marks.
   116	
   117	No crew had ever yet made that book a liar, and its keeper had just set down, in his own hand, the one line that could.
   118	
   119	"Taken," said Pike to the tents, and said the whole slip through again, with that word at the end of it.
   120	
   121	The carter had put his hat on.
   122	
   123	"I go within the hour," he said. "It's my road you'll be mending. The boxes ride for nothing."
   124	
   125	"They rode for nothing last time," said Karis.
   126	
   127	"Then it's a habit." He was already walking back to his mule.
   128	
   129	---
   130	
   131	They were over the saddle before the mist had lifted, with a mark's worth of flour and bacon in the bag and nine marks left in Karis's purse.
   132	
   133	The road was the one they knew. It went down into the grey scrub in two ruts, and the cart fitted them as before; the three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas, and Karis walked at the off wheel. They passed the wash in the middle of the morning, dry and red, with its thorn stump on the eastern lip. They kept the hold without thinking of it, and Lira's knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger could see them. In nearly every particular it was the walk they had made ten days ago for nine marks.
   134	
   135	It felt like no walk Cael had ever taken, and he was half the day finding out why.
   136	
   137	He had gone to a good many fights. He had gone to them down a lane in Ardenmere, and up the steps of a hall at Norhold, and across sixty feet of sprung oak every week for two years; and in every case somebody else had drawn the card. A keeper had matched him, or a bracket had, or a house that needed its charter, and he had walked out onto whatever floor they pointed to and tried to lose as little as he could. Nobody had drawn this one. A slip had been laid on a plank for anyone at all, and he had put up his hand, and reached for it.
   138	
   139	He had expected that to feel like freedom, and it did, a little. Chiefly it felt like four people walking in front of him toward a river.
   140	
   141	That was new as well. On a floor he had only ever had his own hurts to price, and he had grown so used to pricing them that he did it now without thinking, as a carter greases an axle. He could not do it for Lira's feet, or for Brom's hands. He watched Karis's small straight back beside the wheel, and he had no figure for it at all.
   142	
   143	Brom put an edge on the bill at every halt.
   144	
   145	It had been a hedging tool, and its hook had never needed to be keener than a thorn branch required. He sat on the shaft with a whetstone from the smith and drew it along the inside of the curve, slowly, a stroke at a time, and tried the steel on the hair of his forearm, and went on. He seemed no better pleased with it for being sharp; he had the face that he had worn to count his posts a second time.
   146	
   147	"It'll never be a weapon," he said, when he saw Cael watching. "It's the wrong weight, and the hook's the wrong way for anything that doesn't grow in a hedge. But it's what I've got, and I'd sooner it was sharp."
   148	
   149	"You've a knife."
   150	
   151	"I've a knife for cheese." He turned the bill over. "So have you. I've looked at all five of us. There's the one long knife, and she chose it for how it sat on two fingers. Karis has a thing with a horn handle that she sharpens pencils on." He lowered his voice, though the fifth of them was thirty paces ahead on the verge. "And he's got one I've never seen, and I'd bet the forty that it's the best of the lot."
   152	
   153	They camped cold, in the hollow they had used before, well back from the stream. The carter saw to his mule and ate what he had brought, looking at the ground between his boots. When he had done he wiped his knife on his knee.
   154	
   155	"He was Force," he said.
   156	
   157	It was a moment before anybody understood that he meant the man at the ford.
   158	
   159	"A strong one. I never heard what tier, for he never said, and nobody asked him. I've had a wheel go off the groove on that rock four times in twenty-two years, and three of them he was there with his stones. He'd get down in the river to his chest and put his two arms under my tail-board, and it'd come up like a lid." The carter looked at his knife. "In the water. Laughing, the most of the time. He thought it was a great thing to do for a man."
   160	
   161	He put the knife away and went to his blanket.
