   184	
   185	He had crossed the ford three times and never known that it was called anything.
   186	
   187	They came to it in the morning of the fifty-second day, with the sun still behind the eastern hill and the grass grey with dew. The ruts ran down through a gap in the scrub onto a beach of pale stones, and went into the water, and the carter stopped his mule ten yards short and stood with his fist in the cheek-strap.
   188	
   189	"That's her," he said.
   190	
   191	He lifted his chin at the river, and Cael looked where he was shown, and saw at last what he had only waded through before.
   192	
   193	The valley's water was fifty yards wide there, where everywhere else on that road it was twenty, and the reason lay under it. A single ledge of grey rock crossed the bed from bank to bank, ten feet broad and as level as a doorstep, and the river slid over it no deeper than a man's shin, fast and clear, with a skin on it like glass. Two grooves had been worn along the ledge by more wheels than he could guess at, and the rock in them and round them was green.
   194	
   195	Above the ledge the river was a long slow glide. Below it there was a row of white stones, each the size of a loaf, set along the rock's downstream edge at intervals of a yard; and beyond the white stones the water was a different colour.
   196	
   197	"That's the lip," said the carter. "Keep your wheel between the grooves and the white, and you're on the rock. Past the white there's nothing." He pointed with his chin again. "You'll see where he'd got to."
