editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:151:For the first quarter of a mile there was nothing but the noise, and Cael had not known until then how much noise a quiet valley makes. There were frogs. The bottom was wet, somewhere under the grass, and they were in it by the hundred, a creaking that rose and fell like something breathing. There were crickets, nearer, in a dry steady saw, and some small bird that ticked. Over all of it the wind went through a mile of standing grass with a sound like a hand drawn slowly down a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:5:There were a dozen pieces in the sack, and it was plainly the great event of the month. He had never known that meadow so quiet. People had come up from every fire, and they stood about the eave in a loose half ring; and each time the one hand went into the canvas they leaned a little, all together, like grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:27:Cael knew the hand before he had it in his fingers. It was square and upright, with every letter standing by itself, and it had labelled each drawer of a shop in Denvash. He had sent his own letter west on the fourth day with no place on it to answer to, so that it should leave no thread back to an old man with two polite visitors; and the old man had answered anyway, into the air.

